1. What is less?

The less command is used to read a large file one screen at a time. It is useful when the file contains many lines.

=> Practical Examples

Create a sample file:

cat > sample.txt <<EOF
Line 1
Line 2
Line 3
Line 4
Line 5
EOF

Read the file:

less sample.txt

=> Useful keys inside less:

Space — Move to the next page
b — Move to the previous page
↑ / ↓ — Move one line
/word — Search for a word
q — Quit

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to read large files page by page without displaying the entire file at once.

2. What is more?

The more command displays a file one screen at a time. It is useful for reading long files in the terminal.

Practical Examples
more sample.txt

=> Useful keys:

Space — Next page
Enter — Next line
q — Quit

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to read long files page by page using the more command.

3. What is head?

The head command displays the beginning of a file. By default, it shows the first 10 lines.

=> Practical Examples

a) Display the first 10 lines:

head sample.txt

b) Display the first 3 lines:

head -n 3 sample.txt

c) Display the first 20 bytes:

head -c 20 sample.txt

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to quickly check the beginning of a file without opening the complete file.

4. What is tail?

The tail command displays the end of a file. By default, it shows the last 10 lines.

It is commonly used by Linux administrators to check log files.

=> Practical Examples

a) Display the last 10 lines:

tail sample.txt

b) Display the last 3 lines:

tail -n 3 sample.txt

c) Follow a file continuously:

tail -f /var/log/syslog

The -f option displays new lines as they are added to the file.

Press Ctrl + C to stop the command.

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to check the latest lines of a file and monitor log files in real time.

5. What is rmdir?

The rmdir command is used to remove an empty directory.

=> Practical Examples

a) Create an empty directory:

mkdir test-directory

b) Remove the empty directory:

rmdir test-directory

Check whether it was removed:

ls

If the directory contains files, rmdir will not remove it.

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to remove empty directories safely using rmdir.

6. What is rm -rf?

The rm -rf command is used to remove directories and their contents.

-r means recursive
-f means force

=> Practical Examples

Create a directory with a file:

mkdir -p test-directory
touch test-directory/file.txt

Remove the directory and its contents:

rm -rf test-directory

Check whether it was removed:

ls

=> Important Note

rm -rf permanently deletes files and directories. It does not move them to the Trash.

Always check the path before running this command:

pwd
ls

Note:- What I Learned

I learned how to remove a directory and its contents using rm -rf. I also learned that this command must be used carefully because deleted files may not be recoverable.


